Yes, I can certainly help with that. The NAME_1 layout is an SWT layout that positions controls in a grid-like pattern. Here's an example of a simple program that uses the NAME_1 layout:
```java
import org.eclipse.swt.*;
import org.eclipse.swt.layout.*;
import org.eclipse.swt.widgets.*;

public class NAME_1Example {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Display display = new Display();
    Shell shell = new Shell(display);

    // Create a new NAME_1 layout
    NameLayout layout = new NameLayout();
    shell.setLayout(layout);

    // Create some controls
    Text text1 = new Text(shell, SWT.BORDER);
    text1.setText("Hello");
    Text text2 = new Text(shell, SWT.BORDER);
    text2.setText("World");

    // Add the controls to the layout
    layout.add(text1, new GridData(1, 0));
    layout.add(text2, new GridData(0, 1));

    // Open the shell
    shell.open();

    // Run the event loop
    while (!shell.isDisposed()) {
      if (!display.readAndDispatch()) {
        display.sleep();
      }
    }
  }
}
```
This program creates a shell with a NAME_1 layout, adds two text controls to the layout, and then opens the shell. The `GridData` objects specify the grid location and size for each control.

